WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.370
A customer at the corner shop buys a teapot as a present.

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:12.112
The shopkeeper puts it in a box, wraps the box in gift paper, and ties a ribbon around it.

3
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:20.452
Then the rider from Pedal Post, the town's bike courier, weighs it, scans the label, and rides off, quite unmoved by the bow.

4
00:00:20.452 --> 00:00:22.743
A wrapped parcel is still a parcel.

5
00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:25.428
The courier handles it exactly as before.

6
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:29.248
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:29.248 --> 00:00:35.331
If each layer of wrapping just passes the parcel on, does it matter what order the layers go in?

8
00:00:35.331 --> 00:00:36.344
Hold on to that.

9
00:00:36.344 --> 00:00:38.499
We'll answer it with real numbers.

10
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.673
The shop charges for every parcel it sends.

11
00:00:42.673 --> 00:00:44.388
0.50 for each kilometer.

12
00:00:44.388 --> 00:00:45.389
2.00 to start.

13
00:00:45.389 --> 00:00:48.248
3.00 more for a parcel over 5 kilograms.

14
00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.535
And never more than 8.00 in all.

15
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:54.439
The easy way is one big method that knows all four rules.

16
00:00:54.439 --> 00:00:56.324
But these rules change often.

17
00:00:56.324 --> 00:01:01.655
Every change means opening that one method, and hoping the other three still work.

18
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.951
Wouldn't it be nice if each rule were a piece we could add, remove, or move, without touching the others?

19
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:10.554
That's the gift wrap again.

20
00:01:10.554 --> 00:01:14.617
Give each rule its own small layer that wraps the rule before it.

21
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:18.055
In the middle sits the parcel: the price per kilometer.

22
00:01:18.055 --> 00:01:20.742
Around it, a layer that adds 2.00 to start.

23
00:01:20.742 --> 00:01:22.430
Then one for heavy parcels.

24
00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.117
And the cap on the outside.

25
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:25.082
Let's map it.

26
00:01:25.082 --> 00:01:28.219
The parcel in the middle is the first rule, perKm.

27
00:01:28.219 --> 00:01:30.541
Each sheet of paper is one more rule.

28
00:01:30.541 --> 00:01:33.803
And the courier is the code that asks for the price.

29
00:01:33.803 --> 00:01:42.775
It only talks to the outside, and it can't tell how many layers there are, because every layer answers the same one question: what do you cost?

30
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.714
Let's watch one question travel.

31
00:01:45.714 --> 00:01:48.664
A 12 kilogram parcel, going 20 kilometers.

32
00:01:48.664 --> 00:01:53.159
The question goes in, layer by layer, all the way to the middle.

33
00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:55.547
20 kilometers at 0.50 makes 10.00.

34
00:01:55.733 --> 00:01:58.905
On the way back out, each layer adds its rule.

35
00:01:58.905 --> 00:02:00.698
Plus 2.00 to start: 12.00.

36
00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:02.905
It's heavy, so plus 3.00: 15.00.

37
00:02:02.905 --> 00:02:05.387
And the cap says: no more than 8.00.

38
00:02:05.387 --> 00:02:07.180
So the courier hears 8.00.

39
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:08.464
Your turn.

40
00:02:08.464 --> 00:02:11.531
Two more parcels, both going 4 kilometers.

41
00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:14.744
One weighs 2 kilograms, the other weighs 12.

42
00:02:14.744 --> 00:02:16.497
What does each one cost?

43
00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:17.665
Pause and guess.

44
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:24.101
The light one: 2.00 plus 4 times 0.50, so 4.00.

45
00:02:24.101 --> 00:02:26.785
The heavy one also pays the 3.00: 7.00.

46
00:02:26.785 --> 00:02:31.809
Neither one reaches 8.00, so the cap lets both answers through untouched.

47
00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:34.000
Here it is in code.

48
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.401
Each rule is a small method that returns a new price, wrapped around the old one.

49
00:02:39.401 --> 00:02:42.602
In Java, it's a default method on the interface.

50
00:02:42.602 --> 00:02:44.803
In Kotlin, an extension function.

51
00:02:44.803 --> 00:02:50.537
Read the chain from the inside out: perKm is the parcel, and each call adds one layer.

52
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:54.531
The tricky part is the bare cents of d, inside the layer.

53
00:02:54.531 --> 00:02:57.863
It means: first, ask the price I'm wrapped around.

54
00:02:57.863 --> 00:02:59.396
Then apply my one rule.

55
00:02:59.396 --> 00:03:01.661
The price inside is never changed.

56
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:05.149
Now, back to our question.

57
00:03:05.149 --> 00:03:10.433
Let's move the cap one place in, so it sits inside the heavy parcel rule.

58
00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:13.618
Both chains compile, and both read sensibly.

59
00:03:13.618 --> 00:03:16.586
Same parcel: 12 kilograms, 20 kilometers.

60
00:03:16.586 --> 00:03:18.106
Does anything change?

61
00:03:18.106 --> 00:03:19.264
Pause and guess.

62
00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:23.844
Watch the way out.

63
00:03:23.844 --> 00:03:24.304
10.00.

64
00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.758
Plus 2.00 is 12.00.

65
00:03:25.758 --> 00:03:28.666
Now the cap comes first: down to 8.00.

66
00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:32.569
And then the heavy parcel rule lands on top: 11.00.

67
00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:33.820
Well.

68
00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:39.608
That's 1,100 cents, for a parcel the shop promised would never cost more than 800.

69
00:03:39.608 --> 00:03:44.196
The same four pieces, two of them swapped, and a different price.

70
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:48.679
A cap on the inside should really be called capped, for now.

71
00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:52.653
It caps what it sees, and every layer outside it still gets a say.

72
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.619
Jokes aside, the gift wrap predicted this.

73
00:03:55.619 --> 00:03:58.303
A ribbon tied under the paper is hidden.

74
00:03:58.303 --> 00:04:00.585
The outer layer has the last word.

75
00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:03.329
And here the picture breaks.

76
00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:04.592
Paper can only add.

77
00:04:04.592 --> 00:04:08.050
A layer of code can also take away, as the cap does.

78
00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.840
It could even decide not to ask the parcel inside at all.

79
00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:13.610
Now the name.

80
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:22.443
An object with the same interface as the object it wraps, which does one extra thing and passes the call on, is called a decorator.

81
00:04:22.443 --> 00:04:24.465
Each rule here is a decorator.

82
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:28.705
Decorators wrap behavior too, not just arithmetic.

83
00:04:28.705 --> 00:04:35.178
Big Blue Vans has a booking line that's busy twice, and then books, as booking lines tend to be.

84
00:04:35.178 --> 00:04:38.887
One decorator, Logging, writes down every call it sees.

85
00:04:38.887 --> 00:04:41.787
Another, Retrying, tries up to three times.

86
00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:44.173
Put them together, in both orders.

87
00:04:44.173 --> 00:04:46.873
How many log lines does each order write?

88
00:04:46.873 --> 00:04:47.927
Pause and guess.

89
00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:57.199
With Retrying outside, Logging sits inside the loop, and sees every attempt: 3 lines.

90
00:04:57.199 --> 00:05:02.479
With Logging outside, it sees only the one booking that came back: 1 line.

91
00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:05.191
The same booking, BB-1250, both times.

92
00:05:05.333 --> 00:05:09.768
So the inner layer sees every try, and the outer layer sees one answer.

93
00:05:09.768 --> 00:05:11.205
Neither order is wrong.

94
00:05:11.205 --> 00:05:13.204
They answer different questions.

95
00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:17.755
The JDK has stacked decorators like this for decades.

96
00:05:17.755 --> 00:05:28.075
A DataOutputStream, around a BufferedOutputStream, around a GZIPOutputStream: three wrappers around one stream, a place where bytes go, such as a file.

97
00:05:28.075 --> 00:05:32.585
Each wrapper adds one thing: typed writes, buffering, compression.

98
00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:34.516
So, three rules.

99
00:05:34.516 --> 00:05:42.059
One: the order of the layers is part of the behavior, so pin it in a test, as the lab's tests pin all three prices.

100
00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:45.733
Two: keep each layer to one extra thing, so it can move.

101
00:05:45.733 --> 00:05:51.243
Three: when a layer takes away, like the cap, ask which layers should come after it.

102
00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:54.926
So, does the order of the wrapping matter?

103
00:05:54.926 --> 00:05:55.192
Yes.

104
00:05:55.192 --> 00:05:59.712
Every layer passes the call on, but the outer one has the last word.

105
00:05:59.712 --> 00:06:03.701
The same four pieces gave 8.00 one way, and 11.00 the other.

106
00:06:03.867 --> 00:06:04.635
Quick check.

107
00:06:04.635 --> 00:06:08.602
Say the log feeds a chart of how many bookings the shop makes.

108
00:06:08.602 --> 00:06:10.137
Which order do you want?

109
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.978
Logging outside: one line per booking.

110
00:06:15.978 --> 00:06:21.541
To see how often the line is busy, put it inside, where both busy signals show up.
